# PART 0 — Four product notes before you film anything

I read the repo and tried the live site. Three of these are quick; one is an eligibility question you should settle today.

### 0.1 The site still blocks automated clients — and the README advertises a public read API

I was refused at the robots layer on every path, including `https://marque.trade/api/v1/funnel`. The README says:

> **Read API** — `https://marque.trade/api/v1/agents` · `/funnel` · `/marketplace` — Everything the site renders, as JSON, no key.

An agent marketplace advertising a keyless machine-readable API that a machine cannot reach is the most ironic bug this product could possibly ship, and a technically-minded judge will hit it in about ten seconds. This did not get fixed in P10.5A. Check in this order:

```
curl -s https://marque.trade/robots.txt
curl -sI -A "Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; SomeBot/1.0; +http://example.com/bot)" https://marque.trade/api/v1/funnel
curl -sI -A "curl/8.5.0" https://marque.trade/
```

If robots.txt disallows, fix robots.txt. If a non-browser user agent gets a challenge or a 403, it is Caddy or a bot-protection rule — allow GET on public routes and the whole `/api/v1/*` tree. Verify from outside the VPS. **Fix this before the film ships**, because the film will show the API.

### 0.2 All five reference agents are on testnet 97 — the eligibility line says "live on BSC"

The README states the ERC-8004 identities for Bound, Lattice, Sluicegate, Keel and Redcell are on the testnet registry at chain 97. The hackathon's eligibility text reads *"Agents surfaced on your marketplace must be live on BSC."* Your marketplace does index mainnet third-party agents, so you are not in breach — but the four agents on your homepage stat bar under **Warranted** are all testnet, all first-party, and a judge who follows the chain badge will notice.

ERC-8004 registration is gas-sponsored on both networks. Registering the five identities on **mainnet 56** is probably thirty minutes and it removes the question entirely. I would do it. If you do not, the film must not lean on the phrase "four warranted" — and my script does not (see §3, which uses a much stronger number instead).

### 0.3 The GitHub repo description is the word "later"

Top of the page, in the About panel, and in every OG preview when the link is shared: **later**. Change it to the one-liner from the README. Thirty seconds.

### 0.4 The Ledger's human arms are still incomplete

The README says it plainly: *"Agent arms recorded; human arms in progress."* The screenshots show `awaiting the manual arm` and `unscored — blind scoring needs both arms` on all three benchmarks, even though the time and cost columns are now filled.

That is **30% of the TermiX rubric**. Time and cost are not the whole of it; the rubric asks for output quality, blind-graded. **Finish the quality scoring before the film is cut**, because Act III depends on it, and because a film that shows an unscored ledger is advertising an incomplete submission.

One extra note on that page: the human-arm costs read `$41.72`, `$53.66`, `$57.50`. Those are presumably an hourly rate times elapsed time. **Publish the rate in the methodology.** An unexplained dollar figure to the cent looks derived; a stated rate looks measured. It is one sentence and it protects the most important number on the page.

