# PART 1 — Why the baseline film is mediocre

You asked me to be hard on it. The Oathline production benchmark is an impressive *engineering* document and a mediocre *film*, and the gap between those two things is the whole lesson.

Credit where it is due first: the evidence discipline is genuinely excellent. The continuity traps section — never cutting the simulated SELL against the archived BUY, keeping `ADVISORY` on receipt 14 alongside the separate host denial, refusing to manufacture a flawless terminal session — is better ethics than most commercial product films. Keep every bit of that. Everything below is about craft.

### 1.1 It mistakes technical rigour for craft

Read the document and count what it measures: sample counts, LUFS, true peak, caption character limits, colour matrices, tick spacing, CRF values. Now count what it says about *why a viewer would care*. The ratio is roughly forty to one. It is a document about correctness written by someone who never asked whether the film was any good — and it says so out loud: **"No human listening test was performed."**

### 1.2 The scene plan is the software's order, not a story's order

Thirteen scenes in 150 seconds is 11.5 seconds each. That is a slideshow cadence. And the order — sign, replay, deny, stale, refresh, fill, reconcile, verify, tamper, tool surface, limits, close — is the order the *product* works in, not the order an argument works in. There is no question in the viewer's mind that the next scene answers. The one genuinely good instinct, opening on *"This order never reached Binance"*, is abandoned eight seconds in and never returns as a spine.

A film needs one question asked early and answered late. This has a feature tour with narration on top.

### 1.3 The voice choice is the single largest failure, and it was made without listening

Kokoro `bm_george`, a small local ONNX model, at speed 1.02. The document admits the alternatives had "sample files generated; that alone is not evidence of a comparative listening test."

The narrator is the most important quality variable in a narrated film and it was selected on availability. Local small-model TTS has no dynamic range, no emphasis, no breath, and a flat prosodic contour that the audience for this — technical judges who use AI tools daily — will identify as synthetic within four seconds. Four seconds is roughly your entire budget for first impressions.

### 1.4 The pacing method is inverted

They wrote copy, measured it, and then time-stretched with `atempo` up to 1.14× to fit a fixed scene grid. That is fitting the performance to the container. Professional practice is the exact reverse: **lock the narration first, then cut picture to the narration.**

Every downstream problem in the document is a symptom of that inversion — the 0.235-second-per-segment shortfall that accumulated to 146.7 seconds instead of 150, the sample-padding fix, the 1.14 conform guardrail, the caption drift. None of those problems exist if the voice is the master clock.

### 1.5 The resolution chain destroys the thing the film is about

Captured at **1600×720**. Delivered at **1920×1080**. Camera pushes to **1.34×**.

That means the hero footage is upscaled 1.2× to fill the frame and then cropped a further 1.34×, so the pushed shots are showing a 1600-pixel source at roughly 2.6× magnification. The document concedes it: *"Enlarging 1600×720 footage cannot create missing detail."*

For a product whose entire pitch is *readable evidence*, shipping soft, mushy text on every emphasis shot is not a minor craft issue. It is the film undermining its own argument.

### 1.6 Captions are approximated when alignment is free

*"Sentence-measured, phrase-approximated timing, not word-level forced alignment."* Across 46 cues that is visible drift. WhisperX, aeneas and Gentle all do word-level forced alignment locally, for free, in minutes. There is no reason to ship approximated caption timing in 2026.

### 1.7 The music is a splice, not a score

"Undertow" is a fine track under a permissive licence. The edit is first 144 seconds plus the last 8, crossfaded 2 seconds, to hit a duration. There are no hit points, no build into the tamper reveal, no drop under the key line, no relationship between the music and the argument.

The ducking is a single compressor at fixed threshold 0.045 / ratio 3, which will pump on plosives and duck on room tone. And there is **no sound design layer at all** — the document says so. No transition whooshes, no UI ticks, no impact on the seal. Sound design is the difference between amateur and professional more reliably than music is, and it is entirely absent.

### 1.8 The loudness target is wrong for where this will be watched

−16 LUFS integrated with LRA 8 is an Apple Music / podcast target. Judges will watch on a laptop speaker or a phone, likely in a playlist next to other submissions normalised nearer −14. This film will sound quiet and thin by comparison, and quiet reads as amateur.

### 1.9 There is no audience model

Nothing in the document asks where the film will be seen. No cold open engineered for the first three seconds of a muted autoplay timeline. No title card that doubles as a thumbnail. No end card that survives being screenshotted. No short cut for social. It is a single artefact aimed at a viewer assumed to be sitting down, sound on, willing.

### 1.10 The rubric grades the producer's own priorities

Seven categories, none of which is *persuasion*. "Story" at 20% is scored on comprehensibility, not on whether the viewer wants to click the link afterward. You can score 100 on that rubric and produce a film nobody acts on.

