   eight seconds from the picture alone: the number, then the collapse. Test it with the audio
   disabled before you accept the cut.

7. GRADE. A very slight S-curve on the light footage so the paper ground does not read flat, and
   a matched lift on the cockpit shots so the near-black holds detail. Subtle. If it is visible
   as a grade, it is too much.

ACCEPTANCE: a silent picture cut, watched end to end at full size, with a contact sheet of every
tenth second inspected for legibility.

STOP.
```

## V5 — Captions (1h)

```
Read AGENTS.md and MARQUE-DEMO-FILM.md before touching anything.

PHASE V5 — WORD-LEVEL FORCED ALIGNMENT. The baseline shipped "sentence-measured,
phrase-approximated" timing across 46 cues and that drift is visible.

1. Run WhisperX (or aeneas, or Gentle) against the rendered narration WAV to obtain word-level
   timestamps. Align to the known script text rather than transcribing blind, so the words are
   exactly ours.

2. Build cues from the word timings: max 42 characters per line, max two lines, never break
   before a preposition or an article, never split a number from its unit. Cue in on the first
   word, out 120ms after the last.

3. Render burned-in: 34px, white, 1.3 line height, on a near-black panel at 80% opacity, in a
   dedicated bottom band. NEVER over a number the viewer is being asked to read — if a cue would
   cover evidence, move the evidence or move the band for that shot.

4. Deliver a separate SRT. Do NOT also mux a soft subtitle track; the baseline shipped a
   duplicate default text track by accident.

5. VERIFY BY WATCHING, not by checking the cue file. Play the film and confirm each cue lands on
   its word. Sentence-level checking is what let the baseline ship drift.

ACCEPTANCE: burned captions verified in playback, external SRT, zero overlaps, nothing masking
evidence.

STOP.
```

## V6 — Mix (1h)

```
PHASE V6 — Delivery loudness for the web, not for a podcast.

Narration bus: high-pass 80Hz, de-ess, gentle 3:1 compression, then loudnorm to I=-16 as a
  working level.
Music bed: high-pass 45Hz, sidechained to the [key] split of the narration.
Sound design bus: at least 18 dB under narration.
Final sum: amix inputs=3:duration=longest:normalize=0
FINAL DELIVERY: loudnorm I=-14 TP=-1.0 LRA=7.
  -14 LUFS is the web/YouTube normalisation target. The baseline's -16 with LRA 8 will sound
  quiet and thin next to other submissions in a judging queue.
Stereo 48kHz AAC 256 kbit/s.

MEASURE THE ENCODED FINAL MP4, not the intermediate WAV. Encoding changes peaks.
Then LISTEN to it on laptop speakers AND on a phone speaker. Meters cannot tell you whether the
narration is intelligible under the swell at 2:21. Only listening can. If listening is not
possible, say so in the deliverable and leave that judgement unassessed — do not claim it.

STOP.
```

